WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:03.860
The Clown Prince of Crime has been around
for almost eight decades.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:07.020
And anyone who's been around that long is
bound to do something right.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:11.660
It might be by accident, for purely selfish
reasons, or utter insanity, but while it's

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:15.220
hard to imagine, here are some times that
the Joker was actually right.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:21.080
In the DC Comics line-wide event Dark Nights:
Metal, DC's heroes face antagonists from the

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:21.800
Dark Multiverse.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.960
In particular, they're assaulted by evil,
alternate versions of Batman, and perhaps

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:30.999
the most vicious of them all is the Batman
Who Laughs a black leather-clad, bone white-skinned

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Bruce Wayne with a spiked metal headband covering
his eyes.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.710
The Batman Who Laughs is a version of Bruce
Wayne driven mad by neurotoxin after killing

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:41.320
the Joker, falling victim to one last Joker
trap.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:45.760
When Batman confronts his dark echo in Dark
Nights: Metal #6, he has help.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:50.120
The villain overpowers him, but the Joker
appears and shoots Batman Who Laughs from

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:50.620
behind.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.960
Together, Batman and Joker are able to defeat
the darker Bruce Wayne, and Joker doesn't

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:55.480
hold back.

00:00:55.969 --> 00:00:59.720
Though his full reasoning is never totally
explained, the Joker has a vested interest

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:03.809
in stopping the Dark Multiverse Batman from
the start of Dark Nights, in the prologue

00:01:03.809 --> 00:01:04.760
issue The Casting.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.020
Maybe he just didn't want the competition.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.720
After the defeat of the Batman who Laughs,
the character returned in a few issues of

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:16.400
Justice League before getting his own mini-series
alongside another alternate-reality Bruce

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.440
Wayne, the gun-toting Grim Knight.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.820
At the end of Batman Who Laughs #1, the Joker
fools Batman into thinking he's looking for

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:24.440
protection.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:28.820
Instead, the Joker enters the Batcave and
shoots himself, infecting Batman with his

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:29.800
Joker toxin.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:34.460
As he passes out, the Joker warns, "The only
way you'll beat him… is to become him."

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.880
By the end of the third issue, Batman decides
the Joker's right.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:42.480
In spite of Alfred's passionate objections,
Batman stops fighting the Joker toxin and

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.120
allows it to run its course.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:48.560
Batman does defeat the Batman Who Laughs in
the final issue, but loses his battle with

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:49.220
the toxin.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.120
He attempts to beat the now-helpless Batman
Who Laughs to death.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.500
That's when the Joker appears, pointing a
gun at his old nemesis.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.860
He tells him "You're welcome," and fires.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:02.260
Joker's shot stops Batman without killing
him, giving Alfred the chance to clear Bruce's

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:03.420
system of the toxin.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.600
Writer and artist Sean Murphy showed a different
take on the Batman mythos in the mini-series

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:12.020
Batman: White Knight, turning the Batman/Joker
conflict on its head.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:18.060
Like in the 1989 Batman film, this alternate-reality
Joker was originally Jack Napier, though this

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:22.100
one's white skin is the result of make-up
rather than a fall into a chemical vat.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:36.780
White Knight opens with Batman capturing Joker
yet again after an attempted robbery.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:41.659
Batman forces an entire bottle of pills down
the Joker's throat, temporarily curing Jack

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.160
Napier of his psychosis.

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:48.880
A sane Jack Napier is freed Arkham Asylum
after he successfully argues that Batman Gotham's

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:53.400
legal system never tried to handle his mental
illness with treatment, opting for violence.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:57.700
He becomes one of Gotham's most popular citizens,
and eventually helps to save Gotham from a

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:58.320
super-weapon.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:02.580
The Joker of White Knight isn't above dark
means to justify his ends, though.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:08.040
Using the Mad Hatter's technology and Clayface's
unique physiology, Napier secretly takes mental

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.100
control of almost all of Gotham's villains
for what he sees as noble goals.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:16.560
And ultimately, he transforms back into the
Joker at the end of the series, but not without

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:17.560
saving Gotham first.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:22.660
One of the most well-remembered Batman stories
of the 21st century is "Hush" by Jeph Loeb

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:23.440
and Jim Lee.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:28.240
Most of Dark Knight's rogues gallery shows
up in the story: Riddler, Poison Ivy, Killer

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:31.840
Croc, Ra's al Ghul, Scarecrow, Harley Quinn,
and Clayface.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.879
Batman even has a throw-down with a mind-controlled
Superman.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:36.719
And, of course, the Joker's there.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:38.520
But his role isn't what it seems.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:44.600
Batman #613 ends with a gun-toting Joker crouching
over what appears to be the body of Bruce

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:46.340
Wayne's childhood friend, Tommy Elliot.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:50.220
We eventually learn that Joker didn't shoot
Elliot, and he isn't dead.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.440
In fact, Elliot is Hush, the villain of the
whole story.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:58.500
But seeing Joker over an apparently lifeless
Tommy Elliot is too much for Batman; he beats

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.240
Joker bloody and nearly kills him.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.820
It's only the intervention of Jim Gordon that
saves the Joker's life.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:07.920
At least this one time when he tells Batman,
"I'm innocent," he's right.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:14.400
In 1996's Catwoman Annual #3, we visit yet
another alternate reality where Officer Jock

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.820
Bozer a police officer going after the criminal
duo of Batman and Catwoman gets kicked in

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:24.160
the face by Catwoman and falls into a vat
of chemicals, transforming him into the Joker.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:29.500
This world's Joker is Gotham's best cop, and
he's eventually promoted to police commissioner.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:34.280
Joker finds an ally in his pursuit of Batman
and Catwoman in reporter Edward Nigma, who

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:36.400
Batman and Catwoman try and fail to kill.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:41.460
The issue ends with the deaths of both Batman
and Catwoman, as their son Dick Grayson betrays

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.900
them to Commissioner Joker.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:48.160
2019's Batman #75 includes a nod to that alternate-reality
tale.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:52.860
Batman's back-breaking villain Bane has taken
control of Gotham and made the rest of the

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.880
Dark Knight's rogues gallery his lieutenants,
so the comic opens with Detectives Joker and

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:02.380
Riddler investigating crimes and reporting
to the new Gotham police commissioner, Hugo

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:02.880
Strange.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:08.660
In 2017's LEGO Batman Movie, the Joker has
a personal reason to be at odds with the Dark

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:09.160
Knight.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:13.140
Early in the film, Batman assures Joker that
he is not, in fact, his archenemy.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:14.300
It's a harsh breakup.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:16.780
"There is no 'us.'

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:20.500
Batman and Joker are not a thing.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:22.940
I don't need you."

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:29.240
The Joker, enraged at this rejection, assembles
all of Batman's rogues gallery in one massive

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:34.660
Lego villain army, even bothering with D-listers
like Kite Man, the Polka-Dot Man, and Condiment

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:35.160
King.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:39.240
But by the end of the film, as the city is
literally cracking in half, Batman enlists

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.620
the aid of Joker and his villainous army to
help hold the city together.

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:47.360
He's only able to convince Joker to help by
admitting to him that yes, he is Batman's

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:48.020
one and only.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:55.200
"If you help me save Gotham, you'll help me
save 'us'."

00:05:56.020 --> 00:06:00.540
The animated movie Justice League: Crisis
on Two Earths depicts a version of the DC

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.760
Universe where the moral paradigm is backwards.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:07.160
The movie opens in an alternate reality where
instead of the Justice League protecting the

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:10.540
Earth, the Crime Syndicate rules over the
world with an iron fist.

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:13.540
Instead of Superman, there's the unforgiving
Ultraman.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:17.240
Instead of Batman, there's the scheming and
nihilistic Owlman.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:21.800
This Earth's chief freedom fighter is Lex
Luthor, and Crisis on Two Earths opens with

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:26.490
this more heroic Lex and his old friend the
Jester this upside-down world's Joker racing

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:30.210
to escape the Crime Syndicate to make their
way to the world of the Justice League to

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:31.240
recruit them for help.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:36.120
Lex escapes and succeeds in finding and recruiting
the Justice League, but the Jester doesn't

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:37.480
survive the opening scene.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:42.580
Pursued by evil, alternate-universe versions
of Hawkgirl and Martian Manhunter, Jester

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:46.180
knows that neither he nor Lex will succeed
unless someone stops their pursuit.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:50.660
So Jester makes the ultimate sacrifice, killing
himself and the two Syndicate members in the

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:52.560
process, allowing Lex to escape.

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:57.240
If you've seen The Dark Knight, it's likely
you remember the interrogation scene where

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:01.300
Heath Ledger's Joker laughs when Batman suggests
that the Joker wants to see him dead.

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:05.100
"I don't want to kill you!

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:08.100
What would I do without you?"

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:13.650
Some 14 years before that movie came out,
Batman: Legends of the Dark Knight #65 would

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:17.860
begin a story called "Going Sane," which would
show that The Dark Knight was nowhere close

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:20.740
to being the first time the Clown Prince of
Crime would feel that way.

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:24.940
At the end of the issue, Joker is at a loss
when he believes he's finally succeeded in

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:25.880
killing Batman.

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:31.180
With all of Gotham's media reporting Batman
is missing, Joker's belief seems all but confirmed.

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:34.560
In the following issue, he's renamed himself
"Joseph Kerr."

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:39.090
He falls in love with a woman named Rebecca
and is close to tying the knot, but eventually

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:40.760
the Joker persona re-emerges.

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:46.000
In Batman: Legends of the Dark Knight #68,
a flashback scene shows us what triggered

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:46.820
his regression.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:51.550
As the reformed Joker and his fiancee take
a romantic stroll in the woods, Joseph takes

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:55.550
out what he believes is a map, but is actually
a newspaper revealing the missing Batman is

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:56.960
alive and has reappeared.

00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:01.100
The promise of a new life apparently can't
compete with the endless struggle between

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:02.280
the Clown and the Bat.

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:06.840
Joe tells Rebecca he'll be right back, but
instead he retreats into his old persona.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:12.680
In the 1998's Superman: Distant Fires, it
isn't clear if the story's nuclear holocaust

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:17.270
is caused by human error, computer error,
or the world's major powers getting tired

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:18.720
of having a weapon without using it.

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:23.240
Whatever the case, most of humanity gets wiped
out, and in the immediate aftermath, one of

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:26.860
Earth's only survivors Superman loses his
godlike powers.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:31.800
Fighting his way through tribes of cavemen-like
mutants and giant, radioactive animals, Kal-El

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:36.740
eventually stumbles upon a community of survivors,
including some old allies like the Wally West

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:39.820
Flash, Wonder Woman, and J'onn J'onzz, the
Martian Manhunter.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:44.120
More surprising is that the Joker is living
among the post-apocalyptic survivors.

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:48.600
We don't see a lot of Batman's former nemesis,
but what we do see of him is surprisingly

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:49.100
tame.

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:52.400
Nuclear annihilation apparently forced sanity
upon the Joker.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:56.660
When we do see him, he's working hard to support
the community he's joined, like building a

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:59.940
power generator to bring electricity back
to the survivors' lives.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:06.220
In the video game LEGO DC Super-Villains,
the Joker is one of many DC bad guys who temporarily

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:07.740
works on the side of the angels.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:12.709
When the Justice League disappears and is
replaced with versions from an alternate reality,

00:09:12.709 --> 00:09:17.110
Lex Luthor and his Legion of Doom soon figure
out that the likes of Ultraman, Power Ring,

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:19.880
and Johnny Quick aren't the noble heroes they
pretend to be.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:20.540
"You!

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:24.720
You and your team are no-good impostor copycats!"

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:29.320
These new replacement heroes are, once again,
the Crime Syndicate.

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:34.200
But with the Justice League missing in action,
only classic Legion of Doom members like Luthor,

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:37.200
Grodd, Black Manta, and of course, Joker can
stop them.

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:42.389
We eventually learn the Crime Syndicate is
in cahoots with the interstellar warlord Darkseid,

00:09:42.389 --> 00:09:44.960
who's keeping the Justice League captive on
Apokolips.

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:49.920
Eventually the Justice League and Legion of
Doom unite to get Darkseid, his elite soldiers

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:53.680
and the Crime Syndicate away from their world,
presumably so they can get back to beating

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:54.689
each other up again.

00:09:55.689 --> 00:10:01.019
As he does from time to time, the Joker helps
Batman and his allies in the 2008 story "Batman

00:10:01.019 --> 00:10:05.110
R.I.P.," which focuses on a plot by a group
called the Black Glove, which is intent on

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:06.900
destroying the Caped Crusader and company.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:11.500
Assuming that the enemy of your enemy is your
friend, the Black Glove recruits Joker to

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:12.060
help out.

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:16.480
Unfortunately for the Black Glove, relying
on the Joker's loyalty is a dumb move.

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:20.780
Joker begins betraying the Black Glove almost
as soon as their "alliance" forms, killing

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:25.480
several members of the group and ultimately
coaxing the Black Glove to activate the transmitter

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:27.840
that allows Batman's allies to track them
down.

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:29.340
Why does Joker do it?

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:31.280
A lot of it is pure disdain.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:35.850
The Black Glove prides itself on being inspired
the Joker, while the clown sees them all as

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:37.500
being completely amateurish.

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:42.020
He views them as being unworthy of his time,
and he probably sees them as unworthy foes

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:42.780
for Batman.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:46.780
Remember in The Dark Knight when one of Joker's
henchmen tries to unmask Batman?

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:49.860
The crook gets shocked by something hidden
in Batman's cowl.

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:51.100
Joker mocks him.

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:04.660
The lackey is unworthy to even touch the Batman
in the eyes of The Dark Knight's Joker, and

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:08.730
it isn't a stretch to assume the Joker of
"Batman R.I.P." feels similarly about the

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:09.520
Black Glove.

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:14.880
There was a brief attempt at an ongoing Joker
solo comic series in 1975.

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:18.820
It lasted only nine issues, but the series
offers some interesting reading.

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:23.760
Joker's most ridiculous story comes with its
sixth issue, when Joker duels the pipe-chomping

00:11:23.769 --> 00:11:27.589
Sherlock Holmes himself, and the following
issue sees an interesting conflict between

00:11:27.589 --> 00:11:28.960
Joker and Lex Luthor.

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:33.860
In Joker #7, Lex invents a device meant to
steal personality traits from others.

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:37.771
He means to use it to steal the willpower
from the hero Green Lantern, but the Joker

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:39.500
eavesdrops on his fellow supervillain.

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:44.440
He ruins Lex's plans when he crashes into
Luthor's lab and grabs the device himself.

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:49.399
The result is that Lex's genius is transferred
to the Joker, while Joker's insanity forces

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:50.740
itself upon Lex.

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:53.200
Joker is initially fine with the switch.

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:57.180
He fakes his usual insanity around his henchmen
so they don't suspect anything.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:01.520
He monitors Luthor's actions, and Superman's
least favorite citizen seems to have given

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:05.439
up trying to take over the world in favor
of committing random crimes without caring

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:06.680
whether or not they succeed.

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:10.990
But Joker's newfound genius warns him that
both he and Luthor will die from the switch

00:12:10.990 --> 00:12:12.340
if it isn't reversed soon.

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:16.920
Joker designs a device to reverse the personality
switch, thus saving the world from an even

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:18.360
less stable Lex Luthor.

